WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.440
Here is a small population, and the rate it is growing at right now.

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.860
Will tomorrow's rate be the same, or faster?

3
00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:13.663
Look at one individual.

4
00:00:15.003 --> 00:00:17.243
Over day it may give birth, and it may die.

5
00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:21.943
Births minus deaths, per individual, is the per-capita rate.

6
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:29.667
Multiply that per-capita rate by the population size, N. In a closed population,

7
00:00:30.047 --> 00:00:34.327
with no one moving in or out, that product is the population's growth.

8
00:00:35.587 --> 00:00:37.567
This one rule runs the whole film.

9
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:45.000
On the left, plot the per-capita rate against N. First, hold it flat, the same value

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.000
at every size.

11
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:50.003
Now watch the right panel.

12
00:00:51.323 --> 00:00:53.923
Each newcomer soon adds newcomers of its own.

13
00:00:55.143 --> 00:00:57.643
So each day's gain is bigger than the day before.

14
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:03.147
By day ten, the population is far above what steady adding would give.

15
00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:07.847
This compounding is exponential growth, a J-shaped curve.

16
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.280
No real population keeps this up.

17
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.640
As numbers rise, each individual gets less food, space or shelter.

18
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.380
Births fall, or deaths rise.

19
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:25.333
So tilt the line. Let the per-capita rate fall as N rises.

20
00:01:26.673 --> 00:01:29.493
The logistic model assumes it falls in a straight line.

21
00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:34.747
The line hits zero at a size called K, the carrying capacity.

22
00:01:36.287 --> 00:01:40.187
There, births balance deaths on average, so the per-capita rate is zero.

23
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.700
Redraw the right panel with the falling line.

24
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:51.080
The curve rises, steepens, then bends and levels off toward K. That is the S-shaped

25
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.080
curve.

26
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:54.663
Here is a question before we go on.

27
00:01:55.823 --> 00:02:00.783
On this S-curve, at what size is the population adding the most individuals per day?

28
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:13.807
Halfway to K. When the population is small, each individual adds a lot, but there

29
00:02:13.807 --> 00:02:17.967
are few of them. Near K, there are many, each adding little.

30
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:23.130
Multiply the two at every size and plot the product in orange.

31
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:29.750
It makes a hump, highest at half of K. That is where the S-curve is steepest.

32
00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:33.303
At K, the hump comes back down to zero.

33
00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:39.183
Growth stops there because each individual's per-capita rate has fallen to zero.

34
00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:42.807
So the whole story sits in one teal line.

35
00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:45.847
Hold it flat, and growth compounds.

36
00:02:47.247 --> 00:02:49.687
Let it fall to zero, and growth stops at K.

37
00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:54.087
The straight line is the model's assumption, not a measurement.

38
00:02:55.427 --> 00:02:59.827
Whether real populations have a falling line must be tested in the field.
